{"title":"Count Ways To Build Good Strings","difficulty":"Medium","statement":"Given the integers zero, one, low, and high, we can construct a string by starting with an empty string, and then at each step perform either of the following:\n\nAppend `zero` copies of the character '0'.\n\nAppend `one` copies of the character '1'.\n\nThis can be performed any number of times.\nA good string is a string constructed by the above process having a length between low and high (inclusive).\nReturn the number of different good strings that can be constructed satisfying these properties. Since the answer can be large, return it modulo 10^9 + 7.\n\n## Examples\n\n### Example 1\n\n```text\nInput: low = 3, high = 3, zero = 1, one = 1\nOutput: 8\n```\n\nExplanation: \nOne possible valid good string is \"011\". \nIt can be constructed as follows: \"\" -> \"0\" -> \"01\" -> \"011\". \nAll binary strings from \"000\" to \"111\" are good strings in this example.\n\n### Example 2\n\n```text\nInput: low = 2, high = 3, zero = 1, one = 2\nOutput: 5\n```\n\nExplanation: The good strings are \"00\", \"11\", \"000\", \"110\", and \"011\".\n\n## Constraints\n\n- 1 <= low <= high <= 10^5\n- 1 <= zero, one <= low","starterCode":"class Solution:\n    def countGoodStrings(self, low: int, high: int, zero: int, one: int) -> int:\n        pass\n","entryPoint":"Solution().countGoodStrings","parameters":[{"name":"low","type":"int"},{"name":"high","type":"int"},{"name":"zero","type":"int"},{"name":"one","type":"int"}],"returnType":"int","tests":[{"input":[1,1,1,1],"expected":2},{"input":[1,13,1,1],"expected":16382},{"input":[1,15,1,1],"expected":65534},{"input":[1,16,1,1],"expected":131070},{"input":[1,17,1,1],"expected":262142},{"input":[1,2,1,1],"expected":6},{"input":[1,3,1,1],"expected":14},{"input":[1,4,1,1],"expected":30},{"input":[1,5,1,1],"expected":62},{"input":[1,50,1,1],"expected":797922653},{"input":[1,84,1,1],"expected":892516373},{"input":[1,89,1,1],"expected":560523802},{"input":[10,11,1